    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(5))  # F0..F4\n    customers = list(range(13))  # C0..C12\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(79, 8), (72, 83), (0, 54), (55, 43), (77, 66)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(21, 91), (16, 54), (87, 62), (39, 44), (25, 15), (4, 55), (45, 79), (79, 46), (2, 50), (18, 51), (99, 45), (79, 11), (56, 46)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [247.0, 293.0, 262.0, 271.0, 173.0]\n    customer_demands = [40.0, 12.0, 43.0, 49.0, 54.0, 28.0, 30.0, 17.0, 15.0, 45.0, 31.0, 53.0, 40.0]\n    fixed_costs = [341.0, 219.0, 414.0, 175.0, 446.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.69  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Warehouse opening decision and customer fulfillment plan\n\nWe need to decide which warehouses to open and how best to serve our customers across the region. The candidate warehouse sites and their map coordinates are listed in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (79, 8)\n  - F1: (72, 83)\n  - F2: (0, 54)\n  - F3: (55, 43)\n  - F4: (77, 66). Our customer locations, also expressed as map coordinates, are provided in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (21, 91)\n  - C1: (16, 54)\n  - C2: (87, 62)\n  - C3: (39, 44)\n  - C4: (25, 15)\n  - C5: (4, 55)\n  - C6: (45, 79)\n  - C7: (79, 46)\n  - C8: (2, 50)\n  - C9: (18, 51)\n  - C10: (99, 45)\n  - C11: (79, 11)\n  - C12: (56, 46).\n\nOpening any site involves a one-time cost per location; those amounts are summarized in [341.0, 219.0, 414.0, 175.0, 446.0]. A site can only ship product if we choose to open it.\n\nTransportation spend will depend on how far each shipment travels and how much we ship. We will apply 0.69 to shipments, reflecting the rate used for moving product over distance.\n\nEach warehouse has a practical throughput limit; available capacity by site is outlined in [247.0, 293.0, 262.0, 271.0, 173.0]. On the demand side, every customer has a requirement that must be fully satisfied, detailed in [40.0, 12.0, 43.0, 49.0, 54.0, 28.0, 30.0, 17.0, 15.0, 45.0, 31.0, 53.0, 40.0].\n\nOur goal is to minimize total cost\u2014the combination of facility opening costs and the shipping costs required to fulfill all customer demand\u2014while ensuring that no closed site ships, no open site exceeds its capacity, and every customer is fully served. In practice, this means balancing trade-offs: opening more sites can cut average shipping distances and reduce transportation expense, but it increases fixed costs; opening fewer sites saves on fixed costs but may drive up shipping spend and put pressure on capacity at the remaining locations.\n\nThe decision we need is a recommended set of warehouses to open and a shipping plan that assigns customer demand to the open sites in a way that meets all requirements at the lowest total cost. Once we align on the plan, we will present a consolidated view of the expected opening and shipping costs and the service implications for each customer.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Deciding which warehouses to open and how to serve customers at the lowest total cost\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point on our distribution footprint. The task is to determine which warehouse sites to open and how to route orders so that we meet every customer\u2019s needs while keeping total spend in check. The core trade-off is straightforward: opening more sites can shorten delivery distances and reduce transport spend, but it raises fixed costs; opening fewer sites saves on fixed costs but can push up shipping costs and bump into capacity limits.\n\nHere\u2019s the information we\u2019re working from:\n- The candidate warehouse locations are provided as map coordinates in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}.\n- Customer locations are provided as map coordinates in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}.\n- Each site has a defined capacity in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}.\n- Each customer\u2019s required volume is listed in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}.\n- Opening a site triggers a fixed cost detailed in {FIXED_COSTS}.\n- Transportation is charged at {TRANSPORT_RATE}, so longer hauls and larger shipments cost more.\n\nKey business rules guiding the decision:\n- A site can only ship product if we choose to open it.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied.\n- No site may be assigned more volume than its capacity allows.\n- Shipping costs reflect both how far product travels and how much we move.\n\nOur goal is to identify the combination of open sites and shipment assignments that minimizes the sum of facility opening costs and transportation spend, while honoring all capacity and service requirements. The expected outcome is a clear recommendation of which sites to open and a shipping plan that shows who serves which customers and in what quantities, along with total cost and utilization implications."
